1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,708
Cardiac admissions, a mortality model
built from the chart, and one routine

2
00:00:04,708 --> 00:00:06,167
test sitting in the record.

3
00:00:06,792 --> 00:00:08,667
The question is whether adding it helps.

4
00:00:08,920 --> 00:00:10,712
On its own that answer looks boring.

5
00:00:11,170 --> 00:00:12,462
Two thousandths of a point.

6
00:00:13,128 --> 00:00:14,878
Nobody ships a change that small.

7
00:00:15,420 --> 00:00:19,462
But it is an average over a hundred
and forty-four thousand admissions, and

8
00:00:19,462 --> 00:00:20,795
it is hiding a sign flip.

9
00:00:21,420 --> 00:00:24,920
Some of those ECGs were taken
the same day the patient arrived.

10
00:00:25,545 --> 00:00:26,753
Some were three years old.

11
00:00:27,253 --> 00:00:30,462
The model cannot tell the difference,
because nothing in the feature vector

12
00:00:30,462 --> 00:00:32,337
says how old the evidence is.

13
00:00:32,470 --> 00:00:34,970
So the first ask is a
split, not a summary.

14
00:00:35,345 --> 00:00:40,053
Bin every admission by how old its
ECG was at the moment of admission, and

15
00:00:40,053 --> 00:00:41,470
put an interval on each bin.

16
00:00:42,178 --> 00:00:44,053
One detail matters more than it looks.

17
00:00:44,803 --> 00:00:46,678
Resample patients, not admissions.

18
00:00:47,512 --> 00:00:51,762
Patients come back, and an
admission-level split leaks the same person across

19
00:00:51,762 --> 00:00:52,678
the fold boundary.

20
00:00:52,970 --> 00:00:54,220
This is the resampling loop.

21
00:00:54,762 --> 00:00:59,053
It draws patients with replacement,
rebuilds the table from that draw, and

22
00:00:59,053 --> 00:01:01,553
recomputes every bin
inside the same replicate.

23
00:01:02,220 --> 00:01:03,887
Doing it in one loop is the point.

24
00:01:04,470 --> 00:01:08,220
It keeps the bins correlated, so the
difference between two of them is a quantity

25
00:01:08,220 --> 00:01:09,428
you can put an interval on.

26
00:01:09,970 --> 00:01:12,678
The slope against log gap
comes off the same draw.

27
00:01:13,220 --> 00:01:16,345
Four separate error bars
side by side are not a test.

28
00:01:16,928 --> 00:01:17,680
This is.

29
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:18,600
Here is the split.

30
00:01:19,158 --> 00:01:23,992
A same-day ECG adds four and a half
thousandths, and the interval clears zero.

31
00:01:24,492 --> 00:01:29,408
An ECG over a year old subtracts five
thousandths, and that interval clears

32
00:01:29,408 --> 00:01:31,450
zero too, in the other direction.

33
00:01:32,117 --> 00:01:34,492
Worse than leaving the ECG out entirely.

34
00:01:34,992 --> 00:01:37,325
The two middle bins are
drawn hatched on purpose.

35
00:01:38,033 --> 00:01:39,908
They are underpowered, not null.

36
00:01:40,658 --> 00:01:44,283
Their minimum detectable effect is
larger than the effect they would need to

37
00:01:44,283 --> 00:01:49,200
find, so the honest reading is that we
cannot tell, and the chart is required

38
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:51,867
to say so rather than let
you read a trend into it.

39
00:01:51,960 --> 00:01:54,335
Except that comparison is
between different people.

40
00:01:54,877 --> 00:01:58,502
A patient with a same-day ECG
is being actively worked up.

41
00:01:59,127 --> 00:02:04,085
A patient whose newest ECG is two years
old came in for something nobody ordered

42
00:02:04,085 --> 00:02:05,168
an ECG for.

43
00:02:05,835 --> 00:02:08,252
Case mix alone could
produce the whole pattern.

44
00:02:08,793 --> 00:02:12,877
So the revision is to stop comparing
patients to each other, and compare a

45
00:02:12,877 --> 00:02:13,777
patient to themselves.

46
00:02:14,150 --> 00:02:19,108
Train once on the fresh ECG, then score
the same rows again and again, feeding

47
00:02:19,108 --> 00:02:23,067
the model progressively older
ECGs belonging to that same patient.

48
00:02:23,817 --> 00:02:27,150
Same rows, same
labels, same chart features.

49
00:02:27,858 --> 00:02:30,692
Notice the fitted model is
reused rather than refitted.

50
00:02:31,275 --> 00:02:34,317
That is what makes this an
ablation instead of another comparison.

51
00:02:34,317 --> 00:02:39,025
The only thing that changes between
conditions is the age of one input.

52
00:02:39,927 --> 00:02:43,427
Forty thousand admissions, sixteen
thousand patients, and the effect survives.

53
00:02:44,093 --> 00:02:46,677
Same patient, same
admission, same outcome.

54
00:02:47,260 --> 00:02:51,427
Show the model an ECG from that morning
and it gains five and a half thousandths.

55
00:02:51,927 --> 00:02:55,677
Show it one from about five hundred
days earlier and it loses five thousandths,

56
00:02:55,677 --> 00:02:57,385
with the interval excluding zero.

57
00:02:58,093 --> 00:03:01,635
The line crosses into negative
territory somewhere around two weeks.

58
00:03:02,135 --> 00:03:03,218
This is not case mix.

59
00:03:03,802 --> 00:03:05,093
It is the age of the evidence.

60
00:03:05,350 --> 00:03:07,475
The mechanism is a train-deploy mismatch.

61
00:03:08,225 --> 00:03:12,642
The model learned to trust the ECG
because in training most ECGs were fresh,

62
00:03:13,017 --> 00:03:16,933
then it applied that trust to an input
whose reliability had quietly changed

63
00:03:16,933 --> 00:03:17,833
underneath it.

64
00:03:18,308 --> 00:03:23,267
Train the same model on stale ECGs
instead and it stops trusting them, landing

65
00:03:23,267 --> 00:03:25,058
back at chart-only performance.

66
00:03:25,683 --> 00:03:28,142
So the harm is not in old ECGs.

67
00:03:28,475 --> 00:03:29,808
It is in unearned trust.

68
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:33,725
The obvious fix is to tell
the model how old the ECG is.

69
00:03:34,100 --> 00:03:35,000
We tried that.

70
00:03:35,267 --> 00:03:36,392
It did not clearly work.

71
00:03:36,933 --> 00:03:38,392
Every interval crossed zero.

72
00:03:39,142 --> 00:03:42,933
Matching the training age mix to
deployment looked better on the point estimate,

73
00:03:43,433 --> 00:03:45,308
but its interval crossed zero as well.

74
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:49,308
Both are reported as
negative results, not as fixes.

75
00:03:50,058 --> 00:03:52,808
Demonstrating the problem
turned out to be the easy part.

76
00:03:53,010 --> 00:03:53,910
Your turn.

77
00:03:53,968 --> 00:03:57,177
Take a model you actually run
and pick its most important input.

78
00:03:57,760 --> 00:04:00,218
Then ask a question you
probably have not asked it:

79
00:04:00,593 --> 00:04:04,677
how old is that value at the moment
you score, compared to how old it was in

80
00:04:04,677 --> 00:04:05,577
training.

81
00:04:05,635 --> 00:04:08,760
If those two distributions
differ, you have the same setup.

82
00:04:09,177 --> 00:04:13,510
Bin your validation set by the age of
that feature and check whether the effect

83
00:04:13,510 --> 00:04:14,510
changes sign.

84
00:04:15,052 --> 00:04:16,427
Here is a prompt to start with.

85
00:04:16,580 --> 00:04:17,480
Humanitarians AI.
